{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Do not say, etc.</b> It is difficult: granted, the prohibition against saying ״because of their righteousness״ — we find that he contradicts it in his own words; but the statement ״and because of the wickedness״ — behold, he too agrees and proceeds as he says nearby, ״for because of the wickedness, etc.״ If so, why does he command them: do not say? Perhaps it is because he was concerned they might think that the statement ״because of my righteousness״ was forbidden to them only when they do not conclude with ״and because of the wickedness,״ but when they also say ״and because of the wickedness, etc.״ there is no prohibition even if they say ״because of my righteousness.״ Therefore Scripture says: ״Do not say in your heart, because of my righteousness״ — even if you conclude with ״and because of the wickedness, etc.״"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The text reads הֲגַם presumably for הֵן גַּם ('even if'); rendered hyper-literally without emendation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:16:13.524027766Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}